  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nLarge mid level ridge in place over the Rockies and the Plains with a\nband of brisk northwest flow around its periphery from the Upper MS\nValley thru the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley. Initial cluster of\nstorms remaining to our west this morning where the best instability\nis in place. Confidence has increased suggesting that the\nconditional severe weather threat overnight has diminished. \n\nFocus shifts to a couple of disturbances that affect the area in the\nnorthwest flow across the area today. Exact timing is still uncertain\nbut there is a little more of a consensus suggesting that an initial \nMCS drops south-southeast this afternoon into this evening. The \nhighest instability in the strong category is is expected across the \nwestern counties. This is where the best threat for impacts from a \nforward propagating MCS will occur. Damaging winds is the main \nthreat from these storms. Given steep mid level lapse rates and \ninstability in the hail growth zone, large hail is also a threat. \nAlso can't rule out locally heavy rain with the most intense storms.\n\nAdditional storms will likely develop late tonight into early Tuesday \nahead of a surface low and associated cold front. High uncertainty\nexists regarding coverage and placement of these storms which will \nlikely be elevated. \n\nWith the cold front pushing south thru the area temperatures will \ndrop to near normal Tuesday and to slightly below normal for the \nrest of the week.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nWeak high pressure was offering light winds and mainly clear skies. \nThis combined with an increase in low level moisture was leading to \nthe development of area of fog. The best potential for dense fog \nlooks to be along and southeast of I-71. Will issue a special weather\nstatement and continue to monitor this area. The fog will improve quickly\nafter sunrise.",
